\title{Structural and Finite Results for Union-Closed Families,\\with an Information-Theoretic Companion Identity}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 30, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A finite family of sets is union-closed if it contains the union of every pair of its members. Frankl's union-closed sets conjecture asserts that every such family with a nonempty member has an element occurring in at least half its members. We give a self-contained account of several rigorous results around this conjecture. Every nonempty finite union-closed family has a greatest member under inclusion. The presence of a singleton forces its unique element to be abundant by an explicit injection. Combining this reduction with exhaustive residual analysis establishes Frankl's property on every three-element universe. For the full Boolean lattice on $n$ points we prove
\[
 |\Pset([n])|=2^n,\qquad \sum_{A\subseteq[n]}|A|=n2^{n-1},
\]
and hence $2\sum_A|A|=n|\Pset([n])|$; every coordinate is abundant and the average member size is $n/2$. This is the exact Boolean-cube equality pattern associated with Reimer's average-size bound, obtained without entropy. We describe reproducible finite algorithms and, as a companion bridge to information theory, prove that the Euler--Mascheroni constant is the accumulated Kullback--Leibler divergence between exponential laws of successive integer rates. The unrestricted Frankl conjecture remains open.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Let $\F$ be a finite family of finite sets. It is \emph{union-closed} when
\[
 A,B\in\F\quad\Longrightarrow\quad A\cup B\in\F.
\]
Frankl's union-closed sets conjecture predicts that, if at least one member of $\F$ is nonempty, some element occurs in at least half the members. The statement is elementary, but the unrestricted conjecture remains unresolved. Union closure constrains pairs of members, whereas the desired conclusion concerns one coordinate's frequency throughout the family.

We isolate four exact situations. First, iterated union supplies a greatest member and exposes a join-semilattice with top. Second, if a singleton belongs to the family, union with that singleton defines an injection proving abundance. Third, on three points the singleton theorem and exhaustive analysis of the no-singleton residue prove the desired conclusion. Fourth, the full Boolean lattice has perfectly balanced coordinates and exact average member size.

The Boolean-cube calculation contextualizes the equality pattern for the average-size inequality customarily associated with Reimer's theorem. We establish only the exact cube identity, not the general entropy inequality. A final, independent companion result expresses the Euler--Mascheroni constant as a series of nonnegative information divergences. Both themes illustrate how global quantities emerge from controlled local contributions.

\section{Definitions and lattice structure}
\begin{definition}
Let $U$ be finite and $\F\subseteq\Pset(U)$. For $x\in U$, define
\[
 \F_x=\{A\in\F:x\in A\},\qquad d_\F(x)=|\F_x|.
\]
The element $x$ is \emph{abundant} if
\[
 |\F|\le 2d_\F(x).
\]
The family has \emph{Frankl's property} if some element occurring in a member of $\F$ is abundant.
\end{definition}
The active universe is
\[
 U(\F)=\bigcup_{A\in\F}A.
\]
Points outside it have no effect and may be discarded.

\begin{theorem}[Greatest-member theorem]
Let $\F$ be a nonempty finite union-closed family. Then
\[
 T=\bigcup_{A\in\F}A
\]
belongs to $\F$, and every $A\in\F$ satisfies $A\subseteq T$. Thus $T$ is the greatest member under inclusion.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Enumerate the members as $A_1,\ldots,A_m$. Put $T_1=A_1$ and $T_{j+1}=T_j\cup A_{j+1}$. Union closure implies inductively that every $T_j$ belongs to $\F$. The final set is the union of all members, and by construction contains each of them.
\end{proof}

Consequently, a nonempty finite union-closed family ordered by inclusion is a finite join-semilattice with join $A\vee B=A\cup B$ and top $T$. A bottom need not exist because the empty set need not be a member.

\section{The singleton injection}
\begin{theorem}[Singleton abundance]
If $\F$ is union-closed and $\{x\}\in\F$, then $x$ is abundant:
\[
 |\F|\le 2|\F_x|.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Partition $\F$ into $\F_x$ and
\[
 \F_{\bar x}=\{A\in\F:x\notin A\}.
\]
Define $\phi:\F_{\bar x}\to\F_x$ by $\phi(A)=A\cup\{x\}$. Its image belongs to $\F$ by union closure and contains $x$. If $\phi(A)=\phi(B)$, deleting $x$ recovers $A=B$, since neither input contains $x$. Hence $\phi$ is injective, so
\[
 |\F_{\bar x}|\le|\F_x|.
\]
Adding $|\F_x|$ to both sides proves $|\F|\le2|\F_x|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Every nontrivial union-closed family containing a singleton has Frankl's property.
\end{corollary}
The argument is constructive: the singleton identifies the witness, and the injection pairs each set missing the witness with a distinct set containing it.

\section{The three-point theorem}
Let $U=\{0,1,2\}$. Its power set has eight members, so there are $2^8=256$ subfamilies.

\begin{lemma}[Residual no-singleton verification]
Suppose $\F\subseteq\Pset(U)$ is union-closed, has a nonempty member, and contains no singleton. Then there is $x\in U$ such that
\[
 |\F|\le2|\F_x|.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Encode subsets by three-bit masks and families by eight-bit masks. Enumerate the $256$ family masks. Retain a family precisely when it has a nonempty member, excludes the three singleton masks, and contains the bitwise union of each ordered pair of included masks. For every retained family, count included masks carrying each coordinate bit. Exact exhaustive evaluation shows that at least one count $d_x$ satisfies $2d_x\ge|\F|$.

This is a finite proof rather than sampling: every subfamily has exactly one mask and every predicate is evaluated exactly. The residue is also structurally small. Excluding singletons leaves only the empty set, the three two-element sets, and $U$ as possible members. Distinct two-element sets unite to $U$. The empty set must nevertheless be retained in the analysis because it increases $|\F|$ without increasing any coordinate frequency.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Frankl's property on three points]
Let $U$ have three elements and let $\F\subseteq\Pset(U)$ be union-closed with a nonempty member. Then some element occurring in a member of $\F$ belongs to at least half its members.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $\F$ contains $\{x\}$, singleton abundance proves the result. Otherwise the residual lemma supplies an abundant coordinate. The nonempty-member assumption ensures that the witness is taken from the active universe and therefore occurs in a member.
\end{proof}

The decomposition separates a universal mechanism from bounded residue. Rather than using enumeration indiscriminately, it applies a size-independent injection wherever possible and searches only where that certificate is unavailable.

\section{Exact identities on the Boolean cube}
Let $S$ be an $n$-element set. Its power set is a Boolean lattice and is union-closed.

\begin{lemma}[Power-set cardinality]
For every finite $S$ with $|S|=n$,
\[
 |\Pset(S)|=2^n.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Each of the $n$ elements may independently be absent or present, giving two choices per coordinate.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Exact coordinate frequency]
If $x\in S$ and $n\ge1$, exactly $2^{n-1}$ subsets of $S$ contain $x$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The map $A\mapsto A\cup\{x\}$ is a bijection from subsets missing $x$ to subsets containing $x$, with inverse $B\mapsto B\setminus\{x\}$. Equivalently, after requiring $x$, the remaining $n-1$ coordinates are chosen freely.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Boolean-lattice abundance]
Every $x\in S$ occurs in exactly half of $\Pset(S)$ and is abundant. If $S$ is nonempty, $\Pset(S)$ has Frankl's property.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Total subset-size identity]
For $|S|=n\ge1$,
\[
 \sum_{A\subseteq S}|A|=n2^{n-1}.
\]
In a division-free form valid also for $n=0$,
\[
 2\sum_{A\subseteq S}|A|=n2^n=n|\Pset(S)|.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Double-count the incidence set
\[
 I=\{(x,A):x\in S,\ A\subseteq S,\ x\in A\}.
\]
Grouping by subsets gives $|I|=\sum_{A\subseteq S}|A|$. Grouping by elements and using exact coordinate frequency gives $|I|=n2^{n-1}$ for $n\ge1$. Multiplication by two yields the division-free identity, whose two sides are both zero for $n=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exact Boolean-cube average]
The average member size is
\[
 \frac1{2^n}\sum_{A\subseteq S}|A|=\frac n2.
\]
\end{corollary}

The average-size scale associated with Reimer's inequality is $\tfrac12\log_2|\F|$. For the cube,
\[
 \frac12\log_2|\Pset(S)|=\frac12\log_2(2^n)=\frac n2,
\]
which equals the computed average. Thus the cube realizes the equality pattern. The integer incidence identity is the claim established here; the general entropy inequality is not required for its proof.

\section{Algorithms and reproducible computation}
Subsets of an $n$-point universe can be represented by integers from $0$ to $2^n-1$, with bitwise OR representing union. A family is represented by a mask of length $2^n$.

\begin{algorithm}[Finite union-closed verifier]
Enumerate family masks $M=0,\ldots,2^{2^n}-1$. Decode each into included subset masks. Skip it unless it includes a nonempty subset. For every included pair $A,B$, test whether $A\mathbin{\mathrm{OR}}B$ is included. For each coordinate $x$, count included subsets carrying its bit. Report a counterexample precisely when every count $d_x$ satisfies $2d_x<|\F|$.
\end{algorithm}

A straightforward implementation takes
\[
 O\!\left(2^{2^n}4^n\right)
\]
time in the worst case and $O(2^n)$ space. The singleton theorem permits immediate certification of every family containing a singleton. For $n=3$, all $256$ families are tractable. Reproducible output should record total families, nontrivial union-closed families, singleton and no-singleton branches, and counterexample count.

\begin{algorithm}[Boolean-cube incidence counter]
Stream all $2^n$ subset masks. Add each mask's population count to a total and increment the count of every set coordinate. Verify that the total satisfies $2T=n2^n$ and, for $n>0$, that every coordinate count equals $2^{n-1}$.
\end{algorithm}
This takes $O(n2^n)$ time and $O(n)$ auxiliary space.

The top member of an explicitly given family is computed by OR-ing every member. This takes $O(n|\F|)$ bit operations. For nonempty union-closed input, the result must already occur in the family.

\section{An information-theoretic companion identity}
For positive rates $\lambda$ and $\mu$, define the divergence between exponential laws by
\[
 D_{\Exp}(\lambda\Vert\mu)
 =\log\frac{\lambda}{\mu}+\frac{\mu}{\lambda}-1.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Nonnegativity]
For $\lambda,\mu>0$,
\[
 D_{\Exp}(\lambda\Vert\mu)\ge0.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Set $t=\mu/\lambda>0$. The inequality $\log t\le t-1$ gives $-\log t+t-1\ge0$, which is exactly the stated divergence.
\end{proof}

Define
\[
 g_k=\frac1{k+1}-\log\frac{k+2}{k+1},\qquad k\ge0.
\]

\begin{lemma}[Consecutive-rate identity]
For every integer $k\ge0$,
\[
 D_{\Exp}(k+1\Vert k+2)=g_k.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Substitute the rates, use
$\log((k+1)/(k+2))=-\log((k+2)/(k+1))$, and simplify
$(k+2)/(k+1)-1=1/(k+1)$.
\end{proof}

Let $H_n=\sum_{j=1}^n1/j$.
\begin{theorem}[Finite accumulated divergence]
For every $n\ge0$,
\[
 \sum_{k=0}^{n-1}D_{\Exp}(k+1\Vert k+2)=H_n-\log(n+1).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The reciprocal terms sum to $H_n$, while the logarithms telescope:
\[
 \sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\log\frac{k+2}{k+1}
 =\log\prod_{k=0}^{n-1}\frac{k+2}{k+1}
 =\log(n+1).
\]
\end{proof}

Define the Euler--Mascheroni constant by
\[
 \gamma=\lim_{n\to\infty}\bigl(H_n-\log(n+1)\bigr).
\]
This is equivalent to the usual indexing.

\begin{theorem}[Euler--Mascheroni information identity]
The nonnegative series converges and
\[
 \sum_{k=0}^{\infty}D_{\Exp}(k+1\Vert k+2)=\gamma.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The $n$th partial sum is $H_n-\log(n+1)$ by the preceding theorem and therefore tends to $\gamma$. Every summand is nonnegative by divergence nonnegativity.
\end{proof}

Thus $\gamma$ is cumulative information divergence along the chain of exponential rates $1,2,3,\ldots$. This result is independent of the three-point theorem but shares its local-to-global structure.

\section{Applications and discussion}
The greatest-member theorem canonically normalizes the ambient universe to $T$. In computation, membership of $T$ is also a quick necessary check for nonempty union-closed input. The singleton theorem supplies compact, human-readable certificates and should be applied before enumeration. Analogous certificates based on generators or constrained minimal members could reduce larger searches.

The Boolean cube is an exact benchmark for proposed average-size inequalities. Any universal strengthening must accommodate $|\Pset(S)|=2^n$ and average $n/2$. The division-free identity
\[
 2\sum_{A\subseteq S}|A|=n|\Pset(S)|
\]
is convenient for exact arithmetic and avoids floating-point logarithms.

The information identity provides monotone lower approximations to $\gamma$: summing the first $n$ divergences returns $H_n-\log(n+1)$. It also gives a direct interpretation of a classical analytic constant as accumulated mismatch between neighboring probabilistic models.

\section{Limitations and future work}
The unrestricted union-closed sets conjecture is not established. The three-point theorem is complete but finite; singleton abundance covers only one structural class; and Boolean-cube symmetry does not force abundance in arbitrary families.

Concrete next directions include:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
\item Prove Frankl's property for every union-closed family on four points.
\item Isolate and settle the no-singleton residue on five points, combining it with singleton abundance.
\item Analyze union-closed families generated by two sets $A$ and $B$, distinguishing $A\cap B\ne\varnothing$ from the disjoint case.
\item Use the exact coordinate identity $|\Pset(S)_x|=2^{|S|-1}$ as a reusable refinement of Boolean-cube abundance.
\item Determine exact hypotheses under which adjoining the top member preserves an existing abundant witness; cardinality parity may matter.
\end{enumerate}

For computation beyond tiny universes, symmetry reduction under coordinate permutations, canonical representatives, and branch-and-bound pruning are natural. The main mathematical goal is broader: find injections or order-theoretic constraints that remain effective without singleton assumptions.

\section{Conclusion}
Iterated union creates a greatest member. Union with a singleton creates an injection from sets missing an element to sets containing it. Coordinate toggling in the Boolean cube creates a bijection and exact incidence balance. On three points, the injection handles the structural branch and exhaustive finite analysis closes the residue.

These results establish concrete portions of the union-closed landscape while preserving the distinction between proved special cases and the open general conjecture. The companion divergence identity reinforces the local-to-global theme: carefully selected nonnegative increments can assemble into a global constant. Finding an equally universal pairing, increment, or lattice certificate for arbitrary union-closed families remains the central challenge.

