# The Hidden Abundance Lab

A union-closed family keeps the union of every two members. The central question asks whether this simple aggregation rule forces one element to occur in at least half the family. Begin with the [general background on union-closed sets](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Union-closed_sets_conjecture), then experiment directly below.

## 1. Build a family on three points

Toggle subsets and watch closure and frequency respond in real time. A highlighted family is not assumed to be union-closed: the widget lets you see exactly which choices fail.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

Run the exact enumerator next. It visits all $256$ families on three points rather than sampling them.

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the structural singleton proof</summary>
If $\{x\}$ belongs to the family, map every member $A$ missing $x$ to $A\cup\{x\}$. Union closure keeps the image in the family, and deleting $x$ recovers $A$, so the map is injective. Hence no more sets miss $x$ than contain it.
</details>

## 2. Enter the Boolean cube

The full power set is the symmetric benchmark. Every coordinate occurs in exactly half its $2^n$ members, and its average subset size is $n/2$.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:0}}

Use the incidence counter to test the integer identity $2\sum_A|A|=n2^n$ without logarithms or rounding.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the double-counting argument</summary>
Count pairs $(x,A)$ with $x\in A\subseteq S$. Grouping by $A$ gives $\sum_A|A|$. Grouping by $x$ gives $n2^{n-1}$ because the other $n-1$ coordinates are free.
</details>

{{visualization:1}}

## 3. Find the roof of a family

Repeated union creates a greatest member: the union of every set already lies in any nonempty finite union-closed family.

{{algorithm:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the lattice viewpoint</summary>
Under inclusion, union is the join operation. The union of all family members is a top element, so every nonempty finite union-closed family is a finite join-semilattice with top.
</details>

## 4. Accumulate information toward $\gamma$

A companion identity connects consecutive exponential distributions to the [Euler–Mascheroni constant](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euler%27s_constant). Each divergence is nonnegative, and the first $n$ sum to $H_n-\log(n+1)$.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the telescoping identity</summary>
For positive rates, $D(\lambda\|\mu)=\log(\lambda/\mu)+\mu/\lambda-1$. At rates $k+1$ and $k+2$ this becomes $1/(k+1)-\log((k+2)/(k+1))$. Summing telescopes the logarithms to $\log(n+1)$, and the reciprocal terms form $H_n$.
</details>

## 5. What to try next

Move from three points to four, isolate families without singletons, and exploit coordinate-permutation symmetry before enumeration. The open challenge is to replace finite search by a universal injection or lattice principle that forces abundance in every nontrivial union-closed family.

